 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public officers law, in relation to residency requirements\nfor certain peace officers in Monroe county",
    "basePrintNo": "S6540",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6540-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3-b, Pub Off L",
    "lawSection": "Public Officers Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6540\n\nSPONSOR: COONEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public officers law, in relation to residency\nrequirements for certain peace officers in Monroe county\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 1 of section 3-b of the public officers law\nto authorize non-residents of Monroe county to be appointed as peace\nofficers for the society for the prevention of cruelty to animals.\n\nSection 2 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law requires that peace officers for SPCAs (societies for the\nprevention of cruelty to animals) live in the county in which the SPCA\nis registered. In the case of the Lollypop Farm/Humane Society of Great-\ner Rochester, many of its operations have grown beyond the boundaries of\nMonroe County. The Lollypop Farm is chartered to operate in Monroe Coun-\nty and also has a presence in Livingston, Orleans and Genesee Counties,\nyet it cannot have peace officers appointed from any of those or another\nother contiguous county, as the physical shelter is located in Monroe\nCounty. Like many SPCAs, Lollypop Farm has faced a dwindling pool of\npotential peace officers as the need increases because of the increase\nof animal cruelty situations they are called upon to handle. This bill,\nintroduced at Lollypop Farm's request, would ease the requirements\ncurrently placed on appointing peace officers by allowing individuals\nfrom adjacent counties to serve as peace officers. Lollypop Farm's\nrequest is based on the fact they have several retired law enforcement\nofficers with experience and desire to work for the organization that\nwould otherwise be qualified to serve as a peace officer if not for the\nresidency requirement. There is some precedent in the law already for\ndoing so, as Albany, Allegany, Nassau, Rockland and Suffolk counties\ncurrently have a similar provision in law allowing individuals from\noutside the county to serve. By implementing this change, the State can\nhelp Lollypop Farm be more efficient and effective in serving the people\nand animals of Western New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
